 ### Disentangled Appearance Modeling
-A technique to handle inconsistent lighting, transient objects, or weather variations by disentangling scene content from appearance factors, enabling robust reconstruction from unstructured image collections [S2, unofficial]. It introduces learnable per-image latent appearance codes as input to the network alongside samples to explain per-view variability such as lighting, tone, or transient content [S2, unofficial]. This allows the core geometry to be reconstructed while residual factors are absorbed by the appearance codes, enabling rendering with varying lighting/weather by exploring the latent spaces [S2, unofficial]. Introduced in NeRF-in-the-wild, with extensions including GRAF, HaNeRF, and RawNeRF [S2, unofficial].
+A technique to handle inconsistent lighting, transient objects, or weather variations by disentangling scene content from appearance factors, enabling robust reconstruction from unstructured image collections [S2, unofficial]. It introduces learnable per-image latent appearance codes as input to the network alongside samples to explain per-view variability such as lighting, tone, or transient content [S2, unofficial]. This allows the core geometry to be reconstructed while residual factors are absorbed by the appearance codes, enabling rendering with varying lighting/weather by exploring the latent spaces [S2, unofficial]. Introduced in NeRF-in-the-wild, with extensions including GRAF, HaNeRF, and RawNeRF [S2, unofficial]. It is chosen when scene appearance varies significantly across captures due to environmental factors rather than geometry, contrasting with siblings that focus on fixed view-dependent shading or anisotropic material modeling.
 
